Subject: [PATCH] Close a MIDI port before freeing it (editor crash on domain reload)

Entering play mode with two controllers open killed the editor:

    Reloading assemblies for play mode.
    Kontrols.MidiHub:Cleanup ()                        MidiHub.cs:453
    RtMidi.MidiIn:FreeDeviceHandle (intptr)            MidiIn.cs:32
    MidiInWinMM::openPort: error closing Windows MM MIDI input port
        (midiInUnprepareHeader).
    terminate called after throwing an instance of 'rt::midi::RtMidiError'

Cleanup disposes the open ports on beforeAssemblyReload. Dispose goes through
SafeHandle.ReleaseHandle to rtmidi_in_free, which deletes the C++ object; the
destructor calls closePort(), the WinMM backend fails to unprepare the sysex
header the driver has not returned yet, and throws. rtmidi_in_free has no
try/catch -- unlike rtmidi_close_port, which catches RtMidiError and turns it
into the device error flag -- so the exception escapes a destructor through the
C API and reaches std::terminate. The process is gone before Unity can log
anything of its own.

Closing explicitly first leaves the destructor nothing to do, so the throw never
happens: closePort() is guarded by RtMidi's own connected_ flag, and the error,
if any, is swallowed where it is catchable. A port that was never opened is
unaffected -- closing it is a no-op.

 - SafeDispose(MidiBase): ClosePort() then Dispose(), both guarded;
 - used for both port lists in Cleanup() and Reconnect().

This is the failure the surrounding "connected but silent" symptoms came from:
when the same close failure does not abort the process, it leaves the WinMM
device in a state where the next domain's open no longer delivers messages, with
nothing logged anywhere. Recompile, lose the controller, restart the editor,
get it back -- until the next reload.

Package: com.blindspot.kontrols 0.5.1 - Unity 6000.3.10f1
RtMidi: jp.keijiro.rtmidi 2.2.0
Rationale and the full crash trace: see RAPPORT.md shipped alongside.
---
--- a/Editor/MidiHub.cs
+++ b/Editor/MidiHub.cs
@@ -418,13 +418,37 @@
 
     // -------------------------------------------------------
 
+    /// <summary>Closes a port, then frees it.
+    ///
+    /// <para>The order matters and is not cosmetic. <c>rtmidi_close_port</c>
+    /// catches the <c>RtMidiError</c> the WinMM backend throws when
+    /// <c>midiInUnprepareHeader</c> fails on a port whose buffers the driver has
+    /// not returned yet, and turns it into an error flag. <c>rtmidi_in_free</c>
+    /// does not : it deletes the C++ object, whose destructor calls the same
+    /// <c>closePort()</c>, and an exception escaping a destructor through the C
+    /// API reaches <c>std::terminate</c> - the editor process dies on the spot.
+    /// Observed on a domain reload with two controllers open : "MidiInWinMM::
+    /// openPort: error closing Windows MM MIDI input port (midiInUnprepareHeader)"
+    /// followed by "terminate called after throwing an instance of
+    /// rt::midi::RtMidiError".</para>
+    ///
+    /// <para>Closing first leaves the destructor nothing to do, so the throw
+    /// never happens. A port that was never opened is unaffected : closing it is
+    /// a no-op.</para></summary>
+    static void SafeDispose(MidiBase port)
+    {
+        if (port == null) return;
+        try { port.ClosePort(); } catch { }
+        try { port.Dispose();   } catch { }
+    }
+
     static void Cleanup()
     {
         EditorApplication.update -= ProcessQueue;
         EditorApplication.update -= PollNewPorts;
 
-        foreach (var port in _ports)    port?.Dispose();
-        foreach (var port in _outPorts) port?.Dispose();
+        foreach (var port in _ports)    SafeDispose(port);
+        foreach (var port in _outPorts) SafeDispose(port);
 
         _ports.Clear();
         _portNames.Clear();
@@ -447,8 +471,8 @@
         EditorApplication.update -= PollNewPorts;
         EditorApplication.update -= TryOpenPorts;
 
-        foreach (var port in _ports)    { try { port?.Dispose(); } catch { } }
-        foreach (var port in _outPorts) { try { port?.Dispose(); } catch { } }
+        foreach (var port in _ports)    SafeDispose(port);
+        foreach (var port in _outPorts) SafeDispose(port);
         _ports.Clear();
         _portNames.Clear();
         _outPorts.Clear();
